[# Section 8 — tooling: CLI, editors, LSP, tree-sitter, lint, diff,
   cookbook, diagnostics, build integration, doc-gen, playground,
   profiling. Anchors normative depth in
   tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md, tooling/tree-sitter-cx/,
   scripts/gen_docs/, vcx/cmd/.
   File extension: .cxd (data-only). #]

[section id=tooling group='Start here' n=8 title='Tooling'

  [intro
    """The CX toolchain is one binary (`cx`) and a small fleet of editor
       integrations built on top of it. The binary is the same V codebase
       that backs `libcx` — every binding, every CI gate, and every
       editor talks to the same parser, the same canonicalizer, the same
       evaluator. There is no second implementation to drift against.
       This section is the getting-started half of that surface: the
       subcommand catalog, editor integration, the language server, the
       playground, recipes, and build-system embedding. The verbs
       themselves live with the ring each serves — the data verbs (fmt,
       canonical/hash/eq, diff, validate, select, table, projections) in
       [[data-tooling]], and the code verbs (running programs under
       grants, lint, diagnostics, diagrams, profiling) in
       [[code-tooling]]."""]

  [child id=cli n=8.1 title='The cx CLI'
    [intro
      """`cx` is the one-stop command-line interface. It evaluates,
         formats, canonicalizes, hashes, compares, diffs, lints,
         validates, converts, and renders CX documents. Every
         subcommand is a thin shim over the same V core
         (`vcx/cmd/main.v` dispatches on `argv[1]`), so behaviour is
         identical to what every host-language binding sees. This
         page carries the catalog and the Start-here verbs
         (scaffold/demo/lock, the language server); each working verb
         is documented in its ring — [[data-tooling]] for the Ring-0
         verbs, [[code-tooling]] for the Ring-1 ones."""]

    [body
      """The CLI follows a small handful of conventions. **Running a
         file is the default action**: `cx program.cx` parses and
         evaluates it (a pure-data document evaluates to itself).
         Input comes from a file path or stdin; output goes to
         stdout. Exit code 0 means success, 1 means a user-visible
         diagnostic (lint finding, diff delta, validation failure,
         non-equivalent compare), 2 means an internal or I/O error.
         Subcommands that gate on severity (`cx lint`, `cx
         validate`) accept `--fail-on=THRESHOLD` to tighten the
         gate; default is `--fail-on=error`."""]

    [body
      """The CLI surface is exercised by the conformance corpora
         under `conformance/*.cxd` (lint, diff, schema-validate,
         delimited projections, …) run by `make test-vcx-suite` —
         the fixtures are the normative reference where this guide
         and the binary disagree."""]

    [child id=cli-overview n=8.1.1 title='Subcommand catalog'
      [body
        """The full shipped set. `cx --help` lists every subcommand —
           the catalog and the dispatch are generated from one
           table in `vcx/cmd/main.v`, so they cannot drift — and
           every subcommand answers `cx <subcommand> --help` with
           its own usage:"""]

      [table
        [tr [th 'command'] [th 'purpose']]
        [tr [td 'cx FILE'] [td 'Parse + evaluate a document / program (the default action)']]
        [tr [td 'cx fmt FILE'] [td 'Lossless canonical formatter (preserves comments, anchors; normalizes whitespace/quoting)']]
        [tr [td 'cx canonical FILE'] [td 'Strict canonical text (strips presentation; data-equivalent output)']]
        [tr [td 'cx hash FILE'] [td 'SHA-256 hex of the strict-canonical bytes']]
        [tr [td 'cx eq A B'] [td 'Exit 0 iff strict-canonical(A) == strict-canonical(B)']]
        [tr [td 'cx diff A B'] [td 'Semantic diff over strict-canonical forms']]
        [tr [td 'cx lint FILE'] [td 'Style + correctness warnings']]
        [tr [td 'cx validate FILE --schema=S.cxs'] [td 'Schema validation']]
        [tr [td 'cx table VERB'] [td 'Table API surface (info / dump / load)']]
        [tr [td 'cx schema VERB'] [td 'Schema verb family — infer a .cxs from a corpus; export to JSON Schema']]
        [tr [td 'cx tools VERB'] [td 'Agent-tool verb family — project command defs to MCP tool descriptors']]
        [tr [td 'cx xap init NAME'] [td 'Scaffold a XAP project']]
        [tr [td 'cx xap check-surface DIR'] [td 'Surface derivation check — every surface against the composed grammar + enabled features']]
        [tr [td 'cx demo'] [td 'Self-contained showcase, no I/O, < 1s']]
        [tr [td 'cx scaffold KIND'] [td 'Typed, commented skeleton on stdout (config / data / doc / log / table)']]
        [tr [td 'cx lock'] [td 'Generate / verify cx.lock from [?lib] directives']]
        [tr [td 'cx lsp'] [td 'Language server on stdio']]
        [tr [td 'cx diagram FILE'] [td 'Program/data containment diagram (mermaid / svg / png; svg and png need --allow-subprocess for graphviz)']]
        [tr [td 'cx code-diagram FILE'] [td 'Sequence diagram of a CX program']]
        [tr [td 'cx code-tree FILE'] [td 'Evaluation-tree rendering of a CX program']]
        [tr [td 'cx store-serve'] [td 'Serve a cx-store (platform profile only)']]
        [tr [td 'cx store-health'] [td 'Store health probe (platform profile only)']]
        [tr [td 'cx store-rotate-kek'] [td 'Rotate the store key-encryption key (platform profile only)']]
        [tr [td 'cx store-mint-principal'] [td 'Mint an XSP-AUTH principal offline — seed file + [grant …] stanza (platform profile only)']]
        [tr [td 'cx fabric-serve'] [td 'Serve a cx-fabric (platform profile only)']]
        [tr [td 'cx eval FILE'] [td 'Alias of the default run action; prefer cx FILE']]
        [tr [td 'cx select PATH FILE'] [td 'CXPath query over a document — matches in canonical CX, one per line']]
        [tr [td 'cx version'] [td 'Version / build info (same output as -v / --version)']]]

      [body
        """There is **no** `cx parse` / `cx convert` / `cx modify` /
           `cx help` subcommand — parsing and
           conversion ride the projection flags below, updates are
           programs you run with `cx FILE`, and schema checking is
           `cx validate` (`cx schema` is inference and export, not
           validation). Ad-hoc selection has its own verb:
           `cx select 'PATH' [FILE]` evaluates one CXPath expression
           against a document (see below)."""]]

    [child id=cli-scaffold n='8.1.2' title='cx scaffold / cx demo / cx lock'
      [body
        """`cx scaffold KIND` drops a typed, commented skeleton on
           stdout — KIND ∈ `config`, `data`, `doc`, `log`, `table`.
           `cx demo` runs a self-contained showcase (typed
           round-trip, `[table[…]]` → CSV, a CX program) with no
           file I/O and no network. `cx lock` generates or
           verifies `cx.lock` from a project's `[?lib]` directives —
           `--check`, `--update NAME`, `--output PATH`."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx scaffold config > app.cx
           $ cx demo
           $ cx lock --check"""]]

    [child id=cli-lsp n='8.1.3' title='cx lsp'
      [body
        """Starts the language server on stdio (JSON-RPC 2.0 per
           LSP 3.17). See [[lsp]] for the
           capability surface. Editors spawn it on demand — see
           [[editors]]."""]
      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx lsp    # editor connects on stdio"""]]]

  [child id=editors n=8.2 title='Editor integration'
    [intro
      """CX ships first-class editor support for VS Code, Neovim,
         Helix, and any editor that speaks LSP. Three layers stack on
         top of the `cx` binary: TextMate grammar for display
         contexts, LSP for live editing intelligence, tree-sitter
         for structural / injection duty. See
         [`tooling/install/README.md`](../../tooling/install/README.md)
         for one-command install scripts."""]

    [child id=editor-vscode n=8.2.1 title='VS Code'
      [body
        """The extension lives at `tooling/vscode/`. It bundles:
           TextMate grammar (`syntaxes/cx.tmLanguage.json`), language
           configuration (brackets, comments), snippets
           (`snippets/cx.json`), and an LSP client that spawns
           `cx lsp` on demand. No prebuilt `.vsix` is committed —
           build one with `npm run package` in `tooling/vscode/`,
           then install it with `code --install-extension`."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """# Build and install locally
           cd tooling/vscode
           npm install
           npm run package        # produces cx-language.vsix
           code --install-extension cx-language.vsix

           # Or from the bundled installer
           bash tooling/install/install-vscode.sh"""]

      [body
        """The extension keys off `.cx`, `.cxs`, and `.cxd`
           file extensions. LSP client config (server path, env vars,
           tracing) lives in the VS Code settings panel under "CX
           Language". A sample workspace settings file is at
           [`tooling/lsp/vscode.example.json`](../../tooling/lsp/vscode.example.json)."""]]

    [child id=editor-neovim n=8.2.2 title='Neovim'
      [body
        """Neovim integration uses `nvim-treesitter` for structural
           highlighting + LSP via `nvim-lspconfig` (or the built-in
           LSP client on Neovim 0.11+). A sample config is at
           [`tooling/lsp/neovim.example.lua`](../../tooling/lsp/neovim.example.lua);
           the install script
           [`tooling/install/install-nvim.sh`](../../tooling/install/install-nvim.sh)
           installs the signed tree-sitter grammar (with a headless
           load-proof) and writes a lazy.nvim `dir =` spec pointing at
           the in-repo plugin root — LSP included via native
           `vim.lsp.config`."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """# One-shot install
           bash tooling/install/install-nvim.sh

           # Manual: copy the example into init.lua
           cat tooling/lsp/neovim.example.lua >> ~/.config/nvim/init.lua"""]

      [note
        """The tree-sitter grammar is installed via
           `:TSInstall cx` once the parser is available on the
           tree-sitter registry. Until then, the install script
           builds the parser locally from `tooling/tree-sitter-cx/`
           and links it into the Neovim parser-search path."""]]

    [child id=editor-helix n=8.2.3 title='Helix'
      [body
        """Helix consumes the tree-sitter grammar directly and
           connects to `cx lsp` for diagnostics, hover, and
           completion. Drop the
           [`tooling/lsp/helix.example.toml`](../../tooling/lsp/helix.example.toml)
           snippet into your `~/.config/helix/languages.toml`."""]

      [example lang=toml
        """# ~/.config/helix/languages.toml
           [[language]]
           name = "cx"
           scope = "source.cx"
           file-types = ["cx", "cxs", "cxd", "cxl"]
           comment-token = "#"
           indent = { tab-width = 2, unit = "  " }
           language-servers = ["cx-lsp"]

           [language-server.cx-lsp]
           command = "cx"
           args = ["lsp"]"""]]

    [child id=editor-intellij n=8.2.4 title='IntelliJ / JetBrains via LSP'
      [body
        """JetBrains IDEs gain CX support through their generic LSP
           plugin: install "LSP4IJ" (or the Ultimate-edition built-in
           LSP support on 2024.3+), and register `cx lsp` as the
           server for `*.cx`, `*.cxs`, `*.cxd`. TextMate
           highlighting comes from the bundled
           `tooling/syntax/cx.tmLanguage.json` via the TextMate
           plugin. The setup is identical for Rider, GoLand,
           PyCharm, RustRover, and IDEA."""]

      [note
        """A dedicated JetBrains plugin (richer than the LSP-only
           path) is planned; tracked in
           [`ROADMAP.md`](../../ROADMAP.md)."""]]

    [child id=editor-sublime n=8.2.5 title='Sublime / Zed / other LSP clients'
      [body
        """Any editor that speaks LSP 3.17 over stdio can drive the
           CX language server. The minimal configuration is: file
           pattern `*.cx`, command `cx lsp`, transport stdio. The
           install scripts under `tooling/install/` cover the
           common cases; for anything else, the contract is just
           LSP."""]]]

  [child id=lsp n=8.3 title='Language server (LSP)'
    [intro
      """The LSP server provides the same intelligence to every
         editor that speaks LSP. Implementation: `vcx/cmd/lsp.v`
         plus `vcx/cmd/lsp_features.v`. Speaks JSON-RPC 2.0 over
         stdio; no separate binary, no port, no daemon. Run-time
         entry point is `cx lsp`."""]

    [child id=lsp-capabilities n=8.3.1 title='Capabilities'
      [body
        """The server advertises the following capabilities in its
           `initialize` response:"""]

      [table
        [tr [th 'method'] [th 'status'] [th 'notes']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/didOpen / didChange / didClose'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'full document sync']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/publishDiagnostics'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'parse + lint + schema + CXLS static analysis']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/hover'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'element, attr, directive, CXPath']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/completion'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'triggers : / @ $']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/semanticTokens/full'] [td 'shipped'] [td '12-token legend']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/formatting'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'lossless fmt']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/definition'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'cross-include and anchor']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/references'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'anchor + alias + merge']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/documentSymbol'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'outline of elements and directives']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/foldingRange'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'bracket-structured folding']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/selectionRange'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'structural expand-selection']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/codeLens'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'diagram preview per top-level directive']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/signatureHelp'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'directive arg slots']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/inlayHint'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'inferred types']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/rename'] [td 'shipped'] [td 'anchor / alias rename']]
        [tr [td 'textDocument/codeAction'] [td 'advertised, empty'] [td 'capability advertised; the handler returns no actions yet']]]

      [body
        """Implementation lives in `vcx/cmd/lsp_features.v`. The
           CXLS001-004 diagnostic
           emitters per
           [`tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md`](../../tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md)."""]]

    [child id=lsp-diagnostics n=8.3.2 title='Diagnostic codes'
      [body
        """Diagnostics flow from three sources, each with its own code
           prefix:"""]

      [list
        [item """`CXER` **prefix** — runtime / parse errors from the V core.
                The namespace registry is
                [`spec/03-approved/process/governance.md`](../../spec/03-approved/process/governance.md)
                §9.6; the language-core sub-blocks live in
                [`spec/03-approved/core/code.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/code.md)
                §9.4."""]
        [item """`CX-L` **prefix** — lint findings from `cx_lint` (see
                [[lint]]); same codes the CLI emits."""]
        [item """`CXLS` **prefix** — LSP-only static-analysis diagnostics
                that don't have a CLI surface. Currently shipped:"""]]

      [table
        [tr [th 'code'] [th 'severity'] [th 'trigger']]
        [tr [td 'CXLS001'] [td 'warning'] [td 'unreachable [?match] arm (after [else …] or [case _ …])']]
        [tr [td 'CXLS002'] [td 'hint'] [td '[?match] with no [else …] (silent () fallout)']]
        [tr [td 'CXLS003'] [td 'hint'] [td 'sibling [case …] arms with similar [when …] predicates (consolidation suggestion)']]
        [tr [td 'CXLS004'] [td 'error'] [td '[?modify] [set-attr] / [delete-attr] on attribute-step path (compile-time mirror of CXER0100)']]
        [tr [td 'CXLS005'] [td '—'] [td 'retired ([par] now owns its width as a bounded worker pool)']]
        [tr [td 'CXLS006'] [td 'hint'] [td 'unbounded comprehension — a [?for] over a generator with no bounding clause']]
        [tr [td 'CXLS007'] [td 'hint'] [td 'ambiguous whitespace-separated quoted-string body']]]

      [body
        """Codes are stable strings; editor configs may key off them
           to surface custom UI (e.g. fold a CXLS003 cluster into a
           single inlay). See
           [`tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md`](../../tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md)
           for the emit contracts."""]]

    [child id=lsp-hover n=8.3.3 title='Hover'
      [body
        """Hover content depends on where the cursor sits:"""]

      [list
        [item """**Element name** — element-doc from the active
                schema (if any), attribute table, link to the schema
                source line."""]
        [item """**Attribute name** — type, default, schema source."""]
        [item """**Directive name** — one-line synopsis + link to the
                directive page in this guide."""]
        [item """**CXPath path expression** — resolved XPath
                production, live match-count against the current
                document, axis-name doc."""]
        [item """**Layer-2 host idiom** — desugared Layer-1 form
                (Python comprehension, Go filter chain, Rust
                iterator). Implementation calls
                `cxlib.idioms.explain()`."""]]

      [example lang=cx
        """[# Hovering '//section[2]/p[@lang=\"en\"]' shows: #]
           [# /section[2]/p[@lang=\"en\"]                    #]
           [# → child::section[position()=2]/child::p[@lang=\"en\"] #]
           [# → matches 3 nodes in this document             #]
           [# → axis `child::` selects direct children (default) #]"""]]

    [child id=lsp-completion n=8.3.4 title='Completion'
      [body
        """Completion triggers on `:`, `@`, `$`, and `/`. The trigger
           character determines the candidate set:"""]

      [list
        [item """`:` — atom literals, type annotations (`::int`,
                `::string`, `::date`...), directive names (`[?for`,
                `[?match`...), labeled-slot names inside an active
                directive."""]
        [item """`@` — attribute names from the schema or the
                live document."""]
        [item """`$` — variable names from `[?let]` bindings in
                scope."""]
        [item """`/` — CXPath axes + element names + node-kind
                tests."""]]

      [body
        """All completion items carry `data.kind` so editors can
           sort and group them. CXPath items are tagged
           `data.path_axis=true` per
           [`tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md`](../../tooling/lsp/diagnostics.md)."""]]

    [child id=lsp-codeactions n=8.3.5 title='Code actions'
      [body
        """Code actions are quick-fixes the editor offers for
           diagnostics. The server **advertises** the codeAction
           capability but the handler currently returns an empty,
           well-formed list — no quick-fixes ship yet. Quick-fix
           synthesis is wired later, once canonical fix recipes land
           for the common parse and lint diagnostics (removing an
           unreachable `[?match]` arm for CXLS001, inserting an
           `[else …]` arm for CXLS002, anchor cleanups for the
           CX-L003 / CX-L004 lint findings)."""]

      [body
        """Advertising the capability with an empty result keeps
           editors from breaking while the recipes land; nothing an
           editor shows under "quick fix" today comes from the CX
           server."""]]

    [child id=lsp-semantic-tokens n=8.3.6 title='Semantic tokens'
      [body
        """The LSP semanticTokens response is the **canonical
           highlighting source** for editors that consume it.
           The 12-token legend maps directly to TextMate scopes for
           cross-editor consistency:"""]

      [table
        [tr [th 'token'] [th 'example']]
        [tr [td 'namespace'] [td 'module prefixes (cx:, log:, fn:, …)']]
        [tr [td 'keyword'] [td 'directive names ([?for], [?if], [?match]...)']]
        [tr [td 'variable'] [td 'bound names ([= $x …], [in $x …])']]
        [tr [td 'parameter'] [td 'labeled slot names (:caps, :policy, …)']]
        [tr [td 'property'] [td 'attribute names']]
        [tr [td 'string'] [td 'quoted string values']]
        [tr [td 'number'] [td 'numeric scalars']]
        [tr [td 'comment'] [td '# line comments and [; …] blocks']]
        [tr [td 'operator'] [td '|>, =>, ||, ->, !, to']]
        [tr [td 'decorator'] [td '#id, &anchor, *merge sigils']]
        [tr [td 'enumMember'] [td 'atom literals (:paid, :active, …)']]
        [tr [td 'function'] [td 'element / call head names']]]]

    [child id=lsp-debugging n=8.3.7 title='Debugging the server'
      [body
        """Run with `--verbose` and pipe stderr to a file to trace
           incoming methods. The server logs each request, its
           dispatch path, and timing."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx lsp --verbose 2>/tmp/cx-lsp.log
           $ tail -f /tmp/cx-lsp.log
           [lsp] initialize ← capabilities: hoverProvider=true, ...
           [lsp] textDocument/didOpen ← config.cx (1342 bytes)
           [lsp] textDocument/hover @ 12:8 → 'host' element-doc"""]

      [body
        """Smoke-test the JSON-RPC handshake without an editor:"""]

      [example lang=sh
        """python3 - <<'EOF' | cx lsp
           import sys, json
           def msg(o):
               b = json.dumps(o)
               sys.stdout.write(f\"Content-Length: {len(b)}\\r\\n\\r\\n{b}\")
           msg({\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":1,\"method\":\"initialize\",\"params\":{}})
           msg({\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"id\":2,\"method\":\"shutdown\"})
           msg({\"jsonrpc\":\"2.0\",\"method\":\"exit\"})
           EOF"""]]]

  [child id=tree-sitter n=8.4 title='Tree-sitter grammar'
    [intro
      """`tooling/tree-sitter-cx/` is the tree-sitter grammar for CX.
         Tree-sitter is scoped to **structural and embedded-language-
         injection duty** — element / attribute / scalar tokenisation
         + opaque-PI rendering of `[? ... ]` directives. Per-directive
         highlighting comes from the LSP semanticTokens, not from
         tree-sitter."""]

    [child id=ts-shape n=8.4.1 title='Grammar shape'
      [body
        """The grammar (`grammar.js`) covers everything in
           [`spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf`](../../spec/03-approved/formal/grammar.ebnf) at the
           **structural** level: bracketed elements, attribute key=value
           pairs, scalar literals (string / int / float / bool / date /
           datetime / null), sigil-prefixed attributes (`#id`,
           `&anchor`, `*alias`), collection literals
           (sequence `(...)`, map `{...}`, array — see
           [[code#collection-literals]]), and comments (`[# #]` and
           `# line`). Directives — every `[?Name ...]` form — are
           tokenised as a single opaque `(directive)` region; the
           internal labeled slots and bodies are not pre-parsed at
           the tree-sitter layer."""]]

    [child id=ts-queries n=8.4.2 title='Highlight + injection queries'
      [body
        """Queries live in `tooling/tree-sitter-cx/queries/`:"""]

      [list
        [item """`highlights.scm` — structural scopes only
                (element-name, attribute-name, string, number,
                comment, directive-opaque). Per-directive interior
                highlighting is delegated to LSP semanticTokens."""]
        [item """`injections.scm` — embedded-language injection
                for fenced code blocks (`[code lang='rust'
                [| ... |]]`). Recognised injection languages:
                `python`, `rust`, `go`, `js`, `ts`, `java`, `c`,
                `cpp`, `csharp`, `ruby`, `swift`, `kotlin`, `sh`,
                `sql`, `html`, `css`, `json`, `yaml`, `toml`,
                `xml`, `markdown`."""]]
      [body
        """These two are the only query files that ship. Indent and
           locals queries do not exist — auto-indent comes from the
           editor's bracket handling, and `[?let]` scope intelligence
           comes from the LSP, not from tree-sitter."""]]

    [child id=ts-regen n=8.4.3 title='Regenerating the parser'
      [body
        """The committed `src/parser.c` is generated from
           `grammar.js` by `tree-sitter generate`. The grammar
           directory carries its own Makefile;
           `make -C tooling/tree-sitter-cx install-nvim` builds the
           parser and links it into the Neovim parser-search
           path."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cd tooling/tree-sitter-cx
           $ npm install
           $ npx tree-sitter generate         # regenerate src/parser.c
           $ npx tree-sitter test             # run grammar tests
           $ npx tree-sitter parse sample.cx  # smoke-test on a file"""]

      [note
        """There is no repo-root make target and no CI gate pinning
           the committed `parser.c` to `grammar.js` — after editing
           the grammar, regenerate and commit `parser.c`
           yourself."""]]]

  [child id=cookbook n='8.5' title='Cookbook — common recipes'
    [intro
      """Worked examples of CLI compositions that show up repeatedly
         in real use. Pair-and-match — the CX CLI is designed to
         compose along the standard Unix pipe."""]

    [child id=recipe-convert n='8.5.1' title='Filter JSON, project to YAML'
      [body
        """Read JSON, query / filter / transform via a CX program,
           project the result to YAML."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx --cx input.json > input.cx        # JSON in, CX out
           $ cat input.cx filter.cx | cx > result.cx
           $ cx --yaml result.cx                  # project to YAML"""]

      [body
        """The CX form is the pivot — every conversion is a
           round-trip through the same AST. Appending a program to
           its data (`cat data.cx program.cx | cx`) evaluates the
           program against the preceding document."""]]

    [child id=recipe-validate n='8.5.2' title='Validate config before deploy'
      [body
        """Strict-profile validation, JSON output for the CI
           summary."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx validate config.cx --schema=config.cxs --mode=strict --fail-on=warn \\
             && deploy.sh"""]]

    [child id=recipe-diff n='8.5.3' title='Semantic diff between two YAML files'
      [body
        """Cross-format diff: read YAML on both sides, diff the
           canonical CX form."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx diff a.yaml b.yaml --format summary
           3 attributes changed, 1 element added"""]]

    [child id=recipe-hash n='8.5.4' title='Content hash as cache key'
      [body
        """Use the canonical-bytes hash as a cache key in a build
           pipeline. The hash is stable across formatting changes —
           reformatting `config.cx` will not bust the cache, but
           changing a value will."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ KEY=$(cx hash config.cx)
           $ test -f cache/$KEY.bin || compile config.cx > cache/$KEY.bin
           $ cp cache/$KEY.bin out.bin"""]]

    [child id=recipe-csv-parquet n='8.5.5' title='Convert CSV to Parquet via CX'
      [body
        """Inspect and round-trip a columnar payload through the
           Table API; Parquet / Arrow output rides the
           `libcx_arrow` bridge."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx table info data.cxcol
           $ cx table dump data.cxcol --to=cx | head
           [# --to=parquet|arrow defers to the libcx_arrow bridge (Phase C) #]"""]]

    [child id=recipe-lint-tree n='8.5.6' title='Lint a whole tree before commit'
      [body
        """Recursive lint pre-commit, with shared config."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ find . -name '*.cx' -print0 \\
             | xargs -0 cx lint --format=summary --fail-on=warn"""]]

    [child id=recipe-docs n='8.5.7' title='Render the canonical guide'
      [body
        """Build the canonical guide (this document) from sources.
           See [[docgen]] for the pipeline."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ make docs
           # outputs into _docs_staging/

           $ make docs-diff
           # diff staging vs live publish

           $ make docs-publish
           # copy staging → docs/"""]]

    [child id=recipe-ast-bin n='8.5.8' title='Inspect the AST'
      [body
        """Read the JSON AST — useful when debugging how the parser
           shaped a document."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx --ast doc.cx | jq '.name, (.items | length)'"""]]

    [child id=recipe-roundtrip n='8.5.9' title='Round-trip through every projection'
      [body
        """Assert that a projection round-trip is data-lossless.
           `--lossless` XML carries per-value types, so the
           round-tripped document is strict-canonical-equal to the
           original — the identity contract from
           [[canon-cross-format]] as a CI one-liner."""]

      [example lang=sh
        """$ cx --from=cx --to=xml --lossless doc.cx \\
             | cx --from=xml --to=cx - > back.cx
           $ cx eq doc.cx back.cx && echo lossless"""]]]

  [child id=build-integration n='8.6' title='Build-system integration'
    [intro
      """`cx` is built to slot into the build system you already
         have. The CLI returns conventional exit codes, reads/writes
         on stdio, and produces deterministic output — all the
         properties that let it compose with `make`, `cargo`, `npm`,
         `bazel`, and CI systems without surprises."""]

    [child id=build-make n='8.6.1' title='Make'
      [body
        """A typical pattern: per-`.cxs` schema validation rule,
           gated `cx lint` lane, generated outputs as first-class
           targets."""]

      [example lang=makefile
        """CX        := cx
           CX_FILES  := $(shell find config -name '*.cx')
           CX_STAMPS := $(CX_FILES:.cx=.cx.valid)

           %.cx.valid: %.cx config/config.cxs
           \t$(CX) validate $< --schema=config/config.cxs --mode=strict
           \t@touch $@

           .PHONY: lint
           lint:
           \t$(CX) lint --fail-on=warn $(CX_FILES)

           .PHONY: validate
           validate: $(CX_STAMPS)

           .PHONY: check
           check: lint validate"""]]

    [child id=build-cargo n='8.6.2' title='Cargo (Rust build.rs)'
      [body
        """Use `build.rs` to validate `.cxs`/`.cx` pairs at compile
           time. (Schema-to-types codegen is planned design work with
           no CLI surface yet — when it lands, the same step would
           generate Rust types directly from the schema.)"""]

      [example lang=rust
        """// build.rs
           use std::process::Command;

           fn main() {
               println!(\"cargo:rerun-if-changed=config/config.cxs\");
               println!(\"cargo:rerun-if-changed=config/default.cx\");

               let status = Command::new(\"cx\")
                   .args([\"validate\", \"config/default.cx\",
                          \"--schema\", \"config/config.cxs\",
                          \"--mode=strict\"])
                   .status()
                   .expect(\"failed to run cx validate — is cx on PATH?\");

               if !status.success() {
                   panic!(\"config validation failed\");
               }

               // Schema-to-types codegen: planned, no CLI surface yet.
           }"""]]

    [child id=build-npm n='8.6.3' title='npm scripts'
      [body
        """Wire `cx validate` and `cx lint` into `package.json`
           lifecycle scripts."""]

      [example lang=json
        """{
             \"scripts\": {
               \"lint:cx\":     \"cx lint --fail-on=warn config/**/*.cx\",
               \"validate:cx\": \"cx validate config/app.cx --schema=config/app.cxs --mode=strict\",
               \"pretest\":     \"npm run lint:cx && npm run validate:cx\",
               \"prebuild\":    \"npm run validate:cx\"
             }
           }"""]]

    [child id=build-bazel n='8.6.4' title='Bazel'
      [body
        """A custom rule wrapping `cx validate` for hermetic builds.
           Sketch (treats validation as a generated stamp file):"""]

      [example lang=python
        """# tools/cx.bzl
           def _cx_validate_impl(ctx):
               stamp = ctx.actions.declare_file(ctx.label.name + \".valid\")
               ctx.actions.run_shell(
                   inputs  = [ctx.file.src, ctx.file.schema],
                   outputs = [stamp],
                   command = '''
                       cx validate {src} --schema={schema} --mode=strict \\
                         && touch {out}
                   '''.format(
                       src    = ctx.file.src.path,
                       schema = ctx.file.schema.path,
                       out    = stamp.path,
                   ),
               )
               return [DefaultInfo(files = depset([stamp]))]

           cx_validate = rule(
               implementation = _cx_validate_impl,
               attrs = {
                   \"src\":    attr.label(allow_single_file = [\".cx\", \".cxd\"]),
                   \"schema\": attr.label(allow_single_file = [\".cxs\"]),
               },
           )"""]]

    [child id=build-ci-github n='8.6.5' title='GitHub Actions'
      [body
        """Lint + validate as a PR gate. Reusable workflow that
           installs `cx` from the release artefacts, runs the
           toolchain, fails the PR on diagnostics."""]

      [example lang=yaml
        """# .github/workflows/cx.yml
           name: CX checks
           on: [pull_request]

           jobs:
             cx:
               runs-on: ubuntu-latest
               steps:
                 - uses: actions/checkout@v4
                 - name: Install cx
                   run: |
                     curl -sSfL https://cxhome.org/install | sh
                     echo \"$HOME/.cx/bin\" >> $GITHUB_PATH
                 - name: cx fmt (drift check)
                   run: |
                     for f in $(find . -name '*.cx'); do
                       cx fmt \"$f\" | diff - \"$f\"
                     done
                 - name: cx lint
                   run: cx lint --fail-on=warn $(find . -name '*.cx')
                 - name: cx validate
                   run: |
                     for f in config/**/*.cx; do
                       cx validate \"$f\" --schema=config/schema.cxs --mode=strict
                     done
                 - name: cx diff (vs base)
                   run: |
                     git fetch origin ${{ github.base_ref }}
                     for f in $(git diff --name-only origin/${{ github.base_ref }} | grep '\\.cx$'); do
                       cx diff <(git show origin/${{ github.base_ref }}:\"$f\") \"$f\" --format summary
                     done"""]

      [body
        """Copy the snippet as a starting point — the scaffold kinds
           are data shapes (`config` / `data` / `doc` / `log` /
           `table`); there is no workflow scaffold."""]]

    [child id=build-ci-gitlab n='8.6.6' title='GitLab CI'
      [body
        """Equivalent for GitLab CI:"""]

      [example lang=yaml
        """# .gitlab-ci.yml — pin the image tag to the release you target
           cx:
             image: ghcr.io/cx-home/cx:latest
             script:
               - for f in $(find . -name '*.cx'); do cx fmt "$f" | diff - "$f"; done
               - cx lint --fail-on=warn $(find . -name '*.cx')
               - for f in config/**/*.cx; do cx validate \"$f\" --schema=config/schema.cxs --mode=strict; done
             rules:
               - if: $CI_PIPELINE_SOURCE == 'merge_request_event'"""]]

    [child id=build-pre-commit n='8.6.7' title='Pre-commit hooks'
      [body
        """Wire a `cx fmt` drift check and `cx lint` into the
           [pre-commit](https://pre-commit.com/) framework. There is
           no `--check` flag on `cx fmt` — diff the formatter output
           against the file instead."""]

      [example lang=yaml
        """# .pre-commit-config.yaml
           repos:
             - repo: local
               hooks:
                 - id: cx-fmt
                   name: cx fmt drift check
                   entry: bash -c 'for f in \"$@\"; do cx fmt \"$f\" | diff - \"$f\" || exit 1; done' --
                   language: system
                   files: '\\.(cx|cxd|cxs|cxl)$'
                 - id: cx-lint
                   name: cx lint
                   entry: cx lint --fail-on=warn
                   language: system
                   files: '\\.(cx|cxd)$'
                 - id: cx-validate
                   name: cx validate (strict)
                   entry: cx validate --schema=config/schema.cxs --mode=strict
                   language: system
                   files: '^config/.*\\.cx$'"""]

      [note
        """Copy the snippet directly — there is no pre-commit
           scaffold kind (`cx scaffold` emits data shapes only)."""]]]

  [child id=docgen n='8.7' title='Doc-gen pipeline'
    [intro
      """The documentation site (and this canonical guide) is built
         from `.cxd`/`.cx` sources under `docs-src/` by a Make-driven
         pipeline at `scripts/gen_docs/`. The pipeline is itself
         dogfood — the inputs are CX, the templates are CX, the
         outputs are HTML rendered by running the generator with `cx`."""]

    [child id=docgen-make n='8.7.1' title='Make targets'
      [body
        """The top-level Makefile splices `-include
           scripts/gen_docs/docs.mk` to bring the doc targets into
           scope:"""]

      [table
        [tr [th 'target'] [th 'purpose']]
        [tr [td 'make docs'] [td 'build the site into _docs_staging/']]
        [tr [td 'make docs-diff'] [td 'diff staging vs the live docs/ tree']]
        [tr [td 'make docs-publish'] [td 'copy staging → docs/ with backup on divergence']]
        [tr [td 'make docs-clean'] [td 'wipe _docs_staging/']]
        [tr [td 'make docs-check'] [td 'broken-link + missing-anchor verification']]
        [tr [td 'make docs-all'] [td 'docs + docs-check']]]]

    [child id=docgen-staging n='8.7.2' title='Staging vs live'
      [body
        """`make docs` writes to `_docs_staging/` (in `.gitignore`).
           The live tree at `docs/` is touched only by `make
           docs-publish`, which copies staging → live with a backup
           on any divergence. This separation means doc builds never
           dirty the working tree on incidental builds — only an
           explicit publish step lands changes."""]]

    [child id=docgen-canonical-guide n='8.7.3' title='The canonical guide'
      [body
        """This document — the canonical CX guide — is built by
           `make docs-canonical-guide` from sources under
           `docs-src/canonical/sections/*.cxd`. The pipeline:"""]

      [list
        [item """Reads `docs-src/canonical/manifest.cxd` for the
                table of contents."""]
        [item """For each section file, runs `cx` against the
                rendering template, emitting an HTML fragment."""]
        [item """Concatenates fragments, threads the TOC, expands
                `[[anchor]]` cross-links."""]
        [item """Runs the link-verifier — every `[[anchor]]` and
                every relative URL must resolve."""]
        [item """Writes to `_docs_staging/canonical-guide.html`."""]]

      [note
        """Broken links fail the build. `make docs-check` runs the
           verifier without the render — useful for fast iteration."""]]

    [child id=docgen-snippets n='8.7.4' title='Example snippets'
      [body
        """Code examples in the doc sources can pull from
           `conformance/*.cxd` fixtures so they can never drift from
           working code. The `{{EXAMPLE:fixture-id field}}` placeholder
           expands at doc-gen time:"""]

      [example lang=cx
        """[example lang=cx
             '''{{EXAMPLE:cxpath-basic program}}''']
           [output
             '''{{EXAMPLE:cxpath-basic expected}}''']"""]

      [body
        """Outputs are re-recorded from the live `cx` binary, so
           the docs are always pinned to current behaviour. See
           `scripts/gen_docs/helpers/example_expand.cx` for the
           expander logic."""]]]

  [child id=playground n='8.8' title='Playground'
    [intro
      """The CX playground is a browser-embeddable interactive
         evaluator. It runs the same `libcx` core as the CLI,
         compiled to WebAssembly.
         No server round-trip — everything runs in the browser."""]

    [child id=playground-wasm n='8.8.1' title='WASM distribution'
      [body
        """The wasm artifacts are the WASM build of the V core,
           exporting a **subset** of the C ABI: `cx_to_cx`,
           `cx_to_cx_compact`, `cx_to_json` / `cx_to_xml` /
           `cx_to_yaml` / `cx_to_toml`, `cx_to_ast_bin`,
           `cx_code_eval` (plus `_with_len` and `_streaming`),
           `cx_code_diagram` (plus `_with_level`),
           `cx_code_ast_json`, `cx_code_tree`, `cx_canonical`,
           `cx_hash`, `cx_free`, `cx_features`, and `cx_version`.
           The bridge layer lives at `scripts/wasm/cxlib.js`."""]

      [body
        """ABI capability bits the wasm advertises (per
           [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §3):"""]

      [list
        [item """**Bit 31** — `_cx_code_diagram` (Mermaid
                diagram render)."""]
        [item """**Bit 32** — `_cx_code_tree` (interactive tree
                model for the output pane)."""]
        [item """The earlier cap bits the runtime would otherwise
                advertise on native, for the symbols the wasm build
                exports."""]]]

    [child id=playground-component n='8.8.2' title='The <cx-diagram> web component'
      [body
        """`tooling/web/cx-diagram.js` is a self-contained
           web component that renders a CX document as a Mermaid
           containment diagram. Drop it into any page that loads
           the wasm build:"""]

      [example lang=html
        """<script type=\"module\" src=\"/cxlib.js\"></script>
           <script type=\"module\" src=\"/cx-diagram.js\"></script>

           <cx-diagram src=\"./users.cx\" max-depth=\"3\"></cx-diagram>"""]

      [body
        """The component reads `src` (URL or inline `<textarea>`
           content), calls `cxlib.code_diagram()` to produce
           Mermaid source, and hands the result to a bundled
           Mermaid renderer. Bidirectional selection: clicking a
           tree node in the output pane highlights the source
           span, and vice versa."""]]

    [child id=playground-tier n='8.8.3' title='Render tiers'
      [body
        """Three tiers per the gate-17 design:"""]

      [list
        [item """**Tier 1** — browser-only Mermaid render. What the
                playground uses. No external service. Currently
                shipped."""]
        [item """**Tier 2** — an HTTP service that shells out to
                graphviz for richer (DOT-based) renders. Designed
                (the editor / CI integration story) but **not
                shipped** — no service directory exists; the CLI's
                graphviz hop (`cx diagram --format=svg|png
                --allow-subprocess`) covers the richer-render need
                locally."""]
        [item """**Tier 3** — wasm-graphviz inline. Planned
                follow-up. Eliminates the Tier-2 service hop while
                keeping the richer-render output quality."""]]

      [note
        """JSON and XML are **not** valid program sources for the
           diagram — `_cx_code_diagram` accepts CX text only.
           Containment-only relationships, no FK inference. The
           conformance home for the diagram output is
           `conformance/code_diagram.cxd`."""]]

    [child id=playground-embed n='8.8.4' title='Embedding in a docs site'
      [body
        """The minimum a host page needs:"""]

      [list
        [item """A wasm build — `libcx-async.js` (single-file
                Asyncify build, ~36 MB; works under file:// and any
                static host) or `libcx-pthreads.js` +
                `libcx-pthreads.wasm` (~30 MB wasm; needs
                cross-origin isolation for real threads)."""]
        [item """`cxlib.js` — the host-side bridge (~26 KB), from
                `scripts/wasm/cxlib.js`."""]
        [item """Optionally the `cx-diagram.js` web component from
                `tooling/web/`."""]]

      [body
        """A demo page for the diagram component ships as
           `tooling/web/demo.html`; the full playground page (eval +
           diagram + tree) is generated into the guide from
           `scripts/gen_guide/playground/`. Any concept page that
           wants an inline interactive surface reuses the same
           components."""]]]


  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/tooling/* and content/reference/cli.cx
     during Phase B docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24). Compact
     quick-references for the CLI, editor install scripts, the
     tree-sitter grammar, and the conformance/fuzz/bench harnesses. #]

  [child id=tooling-quickref n='8.9' title='Tooling quick references'
    [intro
      """Compact reference snippets for the CLI subcommand surface,
         the editor installer scripts, the tree-sitter grammar
         workflow, and the conformance / fuzz / bench harnesses.
         The primary children above (§8.1-§8.12) carry the
         normative depth; this section is the at-a-glance
         summary."""]

    [child id=cli-quickref n='8.9.1' title='CLI quick reference'
      [body
        """Every subcommand reads from a file or `-` (stdin) and
           writes to stdout unless `--output FILE` is passed.
           Format is auto-detected from the extension or set
           explicitly with `--from FMT` / `--to FMT`."""]
      [example lang=shell
        """# Format conversion (one-shot).
           cx --cx pizza.cx                 # canonical CX form
           cx --xml pizza.cx                # XML
           cx --json pizza.cx               # semantic JSON
           cx --ast pizza.cx                # AST as JSON
           cx --toml pizza.cx               # TOML

           # From-To.
           cx --from=md --to=cx README.md
           cx --from=cx --to=json --compact order.cx
           cx --from=cx --to=xml --lossless order.cx

           # Subcommands.
           cx fmt FILE              # lossless canonical formatter
           cx canonical FILE        # strict canonical text
           cx hash FILE             # SHA-256 over canonical bytes
           cx eq A.cx B.cx          # exit 0 if data-equivalent
           cx diff A.cx B.cx        # semantic diff
           cx lint FILE             # style + correctness
           cx validate FILE --schema=S.cxs
           cx table info FILE
           cx scaffold KIND         # drop a skeleton on stdout
           cx select '//user' FILE  # CXPath query (exit 1 = no match)
           cx version               # build info (same as -v)

           # Run a program (data + program in one input).
           cat data.cx program.cx | cx
           cx --allow-read --allow-write pipeline.cx

           # Run a program against a separate input document ($doc).
           cx --data=input.cx program.cx

           # Include resolution.
           cx --xml --include-root=./conf main.cx     # resolve [?cx include=…]"""]
      [note
        """**Exit codes.** `0` = success (no diagnostics at or
           above the configured `--fail-on` threshold); `1` =
           diagnostics found (lint / validate) or comparison
           returned not-equal; `2` = I/O failure, schema load
           failure, or unrecoverable parse error."""]]

    [child id=editor-installers n='8.9.2' title='Editor installer scripts'
      [body
        """The `tooling/install/` directory ships one shell script
           per editor. Each script writes the editor config,
           registers the `cx lsp` server, and drops the
           syntax-highlighting plugin. The CX language server is
           built into the `cx` binary itself — no separate
           install."""]
      [example lang=shell
        """./tooling/install/install.sh           # interactive — detects editors on PATH
           ./tooling/install/install-vscode.sh    # VS Code only
           ./tooling/install/install-nvim.sh      # Neovim only
           ./tooling/install/install-lsp.sh       # LSP wiring only, no extension
           ./tooling/install/uninstall.sh         # remove everything"""]
      [note
        """**VS Code** — extension at `tooling/vscode`;
           tree-sitter syntax + vscode-languageclient LSP harness;
           supports diagnostics, hover, format-on-save via `cx
           fmt`. **Neovim** — config under `tooling/neovim`;
           install script writes LSP client config and
           tree-sitter grammar. **Other editors** — Helix, Emacs
           (lsp-mode), Sublime Text (LSP plugin), IntelliJ
           (LSP4IJ) drive `cx lsp` directly. Point the editor at
           the `cx` binary; register `.cx` / `.cxd` / `.cxs` as
           file types."""]]

    [child id=tree-sitter-workflow n='8.9.3' title='Tree-sitter grammar workflow'
      [body
        """The tree-sitter grammar lives at
           `tooling/tree-sitter-cx`. It is the source of syntax
           highlighting, code folding, structural selection, and
           lightweight static analysis in every editor. Prebuilt
           artifacts ship inside each editor extension; build
           from source for grammar work."""]
      [example lang=shell
        """cd tooling/tree-sitter-cx
           tree-sitter generate
           tree-sitter parse example.cx"""]
      [note
        """Highlight and injection queries ship alongside the
           grammar (`queries/highlights.scm` and
           `queries/injections.scm` — the only two). Indent, fold,
           and locals intelligence comes from the editor and the
           LSP, not from tree-sitter queries. Queries are versioned
           with the grammar; updates flow through editor extensions
           on each release."""]]

    [child id=conformance-fuzz-bench n='8.9.4' title='Conformance, fuzzing, benchmarks'
      [body
        """The conformance suite at `conformance/*.cxd` is the
           cross-binding contract. Each fixture is a `[case id=…]`
           block declaring inputs (`[in-cx]`, `[in-code]`, `[call]`)
           and an expected output (`[out-text]` or `[out-err]`).
           Every binding implements a runner that walks the fixture
           set; a release is gated on byte-identical output across
           every binding."""]
      [example lang=shell
        """# Conformance.
           make test            # all bindings, all suites
           make test-vcx        # V core conformance
           make test-rust       # Rust binding
           make test-python     # Python binding

           # Fuzzer — structured-random input over the parser.
           # scripts/fuzz_cx.cx, run continuously by
           # .github/workflows/fuzz.yml; run it locally with:
           cx --allow-read --allow-write --allow-random --allow-clock \
              scripts/fuzz_cx.cx

           # Benchmarks — vcx/tests/runners/streaming_bench.v.
           make bench           # full bench harness
           make bench-json      # the same run as machine-readable JSON"""]
      [note
        """Streaming throughput gate: the release gate requires a
           ≥200 MB/s floor on the reference corpus. The gate is not
           green yet — `[?for]` streaming measures around the
           200 MB/s line and `[?map]` at 129.2 MB/s on the current
           tree; closing that gap is active work."""]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/{cookbook,showcase,playground}.cx
     during Phase B docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24). New
     §8.7 cookbook recipes (13 from content/cookbook + 3 from
     content/showcase) and a playground deep-dive (§8.11 fold). #]

  [child id=cookbook-extras n='8.10' title='Cookbook — additional recipes'
    [intro
      """Recipes carried forward from the pre-Phase-B docs
         cookbook + showcase. The §8.7 children above (recipes
         §8.7.1-§8.7.9) cover the most common nine; this section
         adds 13 more focused recipes."""]

    [child id=recipe-extract n='8.10.1' title='Extract a single value'
      [example lang=shell """echo '//server/@host' | cat config.cx - | cx"""]
      [body
        """Appending a query to its data and running the result
           through `cx` evaluates the query against the preceding
           document. The CXPath finds the `host` attribute of any
           `server` element at any depth."""]]

    [child id=recipe-filter n='8.10.2' title='Filter records by attribute'
      [body
        """A one-line program filters by predicate — the CXPath
           predicate `//user[= $_@role 'admin']` selects only
           admins:"""]
      [example lang=shell
        """echo \"[?for [in \$u //user[= \$_@role 'admin']] [yield \$u]]\" \\
             | cat users.cx - | cx"""]]

    [child id=recipe-count n='8.10.3' title='Count records'
      [example lang=shell """echo '[$count //order]' | cat orders.cx - | cx"""]]

    [child id=recipe-generate-config n='8.10.4' title='Generate a config from a template'
      [example lang=cx
        """[env host='db.example.com' port=5432 env='prod']
           [?let [= $env [$first //env]]
             [server host=$env@host port=$env@port
                     workers=[?if [= $env@env 'prod'] [then 32] [else 4]]
                     cache=[?if [= $env@env 'prod'] [then '/var/cache/app']
                                                    [else '/tmp/cache']]]]"""]
      [example lang=shell
        """cat env.prod.cx server.tpl.cx | cx | tail -1 > server.cx
           cx --toml server.cx > server.toml"""]]

    [child id=recipe-write-stream n='8.10.5' title='Write a stream'
      [example lang=python
        """with cx.stream_writer(open("out.cx", "wb")) as w:
               for record in fetch_records():
                   w.write(record)"""]]

    [child id=recipe-compose-include n='8.10.6' title='Compose a document from files'
      [; check=none — [?cx include] is approved surface (code.md §13); the
         current binary rejects every [?cx …] directive, tracked as engine
         regression #421. Re-enable the check when #421 lands. ]
      [example lang=cx check=none
        """[server
             [?cx include=lib/defaults.cx]
             port=8080
             log-level=info]"""]
      [example lang=shell """cx --cx --include-root=./conf server.cx > resolved.cx"""]]

    [child id=recipe-render-template n='8.10.7' title='Render a template with includes'
      [; check=none — [?cx include] is approved surface (code.md §13); the
         current binary rejects every [?cx …] directive (#421). ]
      [example lang=cx check=none
        """[page
             [?cx include=partials/header.cx]
             [?for [in $item //order/line] [yield [li $item@sku]]]
             [?cx include=partials/footer.cx]]"""]
      [example lang=shell """cx --cx --include-root=./templates page.cx"""]]

    [child id=recipe-read-parquet n='8.10.8' title='Read a Parquet file into CX'
      [example lang=shell """cx table load orders.parquet --to=cx > orders.cx"""]]

    [child id=recipe-anchor-merge n='8.10.9' title='Share defaults across records'
      [example lang=cx
        """[defaults &shared timeout=30 retries=3]
           [server *shared name=api]
           [server *shared name=worker retries=5]"""]
      [body
        """`&` declares an anchor; `*` merges from it. Local
           fields override; missing ones inherit."""]]

    [child id=recipe-html-safe n='8.10.10' title='HTML-safe templating'
      [body
        """There is **no** `[?cx output-target=html]` escaping mode —
           the key is **reserved and refused** (the pragma registry is
           closed; it once passed through inert while reading like an
           XSS defense). Until context-aware escaping ships, escape at
           the boundary: `[$strings:escape-html]` for text,
           `[$html:sanitize]` for untrusted markup, and
           `[$html:serialize]` for trees built as data — escaping by
           construction, never string concatenation."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/strings']
           [$strings:escape-html '<script>alert(1)</script>']"""]]

    [child id=recipe-log-structured n='8.10.11' title='Emit structured log records'
      [example lang=cx
        """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/log']
           [$log:info 'sale' fields={pizza: 'Margherita', price: 12}]
           [$log:warn 'low-stock' fields={pizza: 'Diavola', stock: 2}]"""]
      [body
        """The `log` module emits structured records to stderr
           (default) or a configured sink. Fields are a map;
           level is one of `trace` / `debug` / `info` / `warn` /
           `error`."""]]

    [child id=recipe-logfmt-parser n='8.10.12' title='Logfmt parser in CX code'
      [body
        """Take a logfmt-style line of key=value records and
           split it into structured CX."""]
      [example lang=text """ts=2026-05-20T18:42:00Z level=info request_id=abc123 msg=ok"""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/strings']
           [?let [= $input 'ts=2026-05-20T18:42:00Z level=info msg=ok']
             [event
               [?for [in $kv [$strings:split $input ' ']] [yield
                 [?let [= $pair [$strings:split $kv '=']]
                   [pair name=[$nth $pair 1] value=[$nth $pair 2]]]]]]]"""]]

    [child id=recipe-build-system n='8.10.13' title='Build system in CX'
      [body
        """Targets, depends, run — declared in CX, evaluated by
           a thirty-line CX code driver."""]
      [example lang=cx
        """[targets
             [target name=all  depends='lib bin']
             [target name=lib  depends='src/foo.o src/bar.o'  run='ar rcs lib.a src/foo.o src/bar.o']
             [target name=bin  depends='main.o lib'           run='cc -o bin main.o lib.a']]"""]
      [; check=none — illustrative driver: recursion over the target
         graph plus shell execution via cx-stdlib/process (requires
         --allow-subprocess and the target commands on PATH). ]
      [example lang=cx check=none
        """[?lib 'cx-stdlib/strings']
           [?lib 'cx-stdlib/process']
           [?def build impure ($name)
             [?for [in $t //target[= $_@name $name]] [yield
               ([?for [in $d [$strings:split [$string $t@depends] ' ']]
                  [yield [$build $d]]],
                [?if $t@run
                  [then [$process:run ('sh', '-c', [$string $t@run])]]
                  [else ()]])]]]
           [$build 'all']"""]]]

  [child id=playground-detail n='8.11' title='Playground — visualize, offline, highlight'
    [intro
      """Deep-dive on the in-browser playground UI. Section §8.11 above frames the WASM / web
         component / tier story; this section is the operator
         reference for the Tree / Graph / Source-diagram bridge."""]

    [child id=pg-how n='8.11.1' title='How it works'
      [body
        """The playground runs entirely in the browser. Pick a
           starter example from the dropdown, edit it in the
           Source pane, and the three output tabs underneath
           show the canonical CX form, the JSON projection, and
           the XML projection of the parsed tree. No server, no
           signup. Twelve starter examples ship in the corpus
           — three data shapes (atom, nested element, whole-shop)
           and nine program snippets covering the
           directive surface."""]]

    [child id=pg-visualize n='8.11.2' title='Visualize'
      [body
        """The Source pane carries a Diagram toggle in its
           header; the Output pane carries a Tree | Graph view
           toggle adjacent to the CX | JSON | XML projection
           tabs. Tree is the default for every
           fresh source — Graph is opt-in."""]
      [body
        """The Source-pane diagram renders the program as a
           Mermaid graph in a sibling pane. The renderer runs
           entirely in the browser via the libcx wasm build — Mermaid text
           emitted by `cx_code_diagram`, then rendered to inline
           SVG by Mermaid.js. Zoom, fit-to-pane, and pan controls
           layer on top."""]
      [body
        """The Output-pane Tree view renders the parsed value as
           an interactive containment-only tree — folder icons
           for elements with body, file-text icons for prose
           leaves, tag and at-sign rows for elements and
           attributes. Built from `cx_code_tree(source)` whose
           JSON contract carries a `{kind, name?,
           value?, loc:{start,end}, children?}` record per node.
           Search, count badges, and standard tree keyboard
           navigation come along for free."""]
      [body
        """The Output-pane Graph view auto-detects mode: sources whose top-level program contains at
           least one `[?directive]` render as a Control-Flow
           Graph (basic blocks; diamonds for `[?if]`; dispatcher
           round-rects with per-arm edges for `[?match]`;
           loop-boxes for `[?for]` with `:bind $x` labels; one
           update-block per `[?modify]`); pure-data sources
           render as an Entity-Relationship Diagram with
           per-instance cardinality inference (`||--o{` for
           repeating children, `||--||` for singletons). Every
           `[?def]` body is its own Mermaid sub-graph."""]
      [note
        """**Bidirectional selection bridge**.
           Click a tree node and the source-pane editor selects
           bytes `[loc.start, loc.end]` and scrolls them into
           view. Move the caret in the source pane and the tree
           pane binary-searches its loc index for the deepest
           containing node and scrolls/selects it. Visualizations
           re-render on Run only — no live debounce — to keep
           the playground predictable."""]]

    [child id=pg-offline n='8.11.3' title='Offline use'
      [body
        """The browser playground is a static page. Clone the
           repo, render the guide with `make guide`, and serve
           `docs/guide/` from any HTTP server (or open via
           `file://`) — the playground works without an internet
           connection. The example corpus, the TypeScript
           bundle, and the CSS all live under
           `scripts/gen_guide/playground/`."""]
      [body
        """That covers the diagrams too. Both halves of the page
           are local: the wasm engine is built `SINGLE_FILE`, so
           it carries its own `.wasm` inline, and the Mermaid
           renderer is vendored at a pinned version under
           `scripts/gen_guide/playground/vendor/` rather than
           fetched from a CDN. Nothing on the page reaches the
           network at load, so the Graph pane draws on a plane
           or behind a filtering proxy exactly as it does
           online — and the bytes that render a diagram move
           only when the pin does, never under a released tag."""]
      [note
        """The one remaining remote reference is the Google Fonts
           stylesheet, shared with every other guide page. It
           degrades cleanly: offline you get the local monospace
           fallback and a fully working playground."""]]

    [child id=pg-highlight n='8.11.4' title='Syntax highlighting'
      [body
        """Every code block on the guide is syntax-highlighted by
           a small in-house JS module at
           `scripts/gen_guide/highlight/`. It speaks the
           languages the docs actually use — `cx`, `shell`,
           `python`, `go`, `rust`, `javascript`, `typescript`,
           `java`, `c#`, `ruby`, `kotlin`, `swift`, `v`, `sql`,
           `ebnf`, `xml`, `html`, `json`, `yaml`, `toml`, `md`.
           The highlighter is regex-based, around 150 lines, no
           external library. The Playground panes use the same
           module on every Run."""]]]]
